  ],
  "text": "Touch something hot and your hand is already moving before you know it is hot. It feels instant. It is not. Everything you feel had to travel first. Nerve signals run at up to a hundred and twenty metres a second. That speed comes from a fatty sheath wrapped around the nerve, like insulation on a cable. Bare unwrapped nerves crawl by comparison, which is why a dull ache arrives late. But the fast signal does not go to your brain first. It reaches your spinal cord, and the spinal cord sends the order straight back down. Your arm is already retracting. The message to your brain is still in transit. So you never decided to pull away. You found out that you had. By the time you show up, your body has already handled it. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
